aaab
aaac
aaba
...

you may encounter:

bcaa
aacc
cbab
abca
...

This can be useful when you want the search to sample
different regions of the search space rather than following
one predictable sequence.

Random does NOT mean infinite randomness. PASSGEN still
operates within the defined search space.


------------------------------------------------------------
12. ROTATION
------------------------------------------------------------

Rotation means PASSGEN explores combinations while also
considering rotated arrangements of characters.

For example:

abc

Rotations include:

abc
bca
cab

The traversal engine uses these relationships when deciding
which candidates to explore.

This can cause candidates with different character
arrangements to appear earlier than they would under simple
sequential traversal.


------------------------------------------------------------
13. PRIORITY
------------------------------------------------------------

Priority attempts to examine candidates according to a
ranking score.

The ranking considers properties such as:

- character diversity
- transitions between characters
- repeated characters
- digits
- symbols
- vowels
- other scoring characteristics

Example:

aaaa

has very little character diversity.

abcd

has much greater character diversity.

The priority traversal can therefore rank these candidates
differently from a simple sequential traversal.

Priority does NOT guarantee that a candidate is more likely
to be a real password. It only means the generator's scoring
system places it earlier in the traversal.


------------------------------------------------------------
14. REPEATED PATTERNS
------------------------------------------------------------

Repeated patterns allow PASSGEN to build candidates by
repeating an existing candidate.

Example:

Candidate:

abc

